cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Motorsport Strategy Lab: cross-championship race-strategy models and a retrospective decision audit"
message: >-
  If you use this software, data or its results, please cite it. The DOI below
  is version-specific and frozen at v1.2.0, which is what a citation should
  name. To point at the project rather than at one release, use the concept DOI
  10.5281/zenodo.22726130, which always resolves to the newest version.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Medjadj
    given-names: Mohammed Reda
repository-code: "https://github.com/mohammedmedjadj/Motorsport-Strategy-Lab"
url: "https://mohammedmedjadj.github.io/Motorsport-Strategy-Lab/"
abstract: >-
  Race-strategy models are typically fitted and validated within one
  championship, often within one race. This work applies one protocol across
  four championships and seven car classes — Formula 1, WEC, IMSA and ELMS —
  and asks two questions the existing literature rarely does: does a fitted
  degradation model predict a season it has never seen, and does its
  recommendation resemble what a professional team actually did.

  Transfer is mostly not measurable from public timing: inside a stint the
  fuel burned nearly cancels the tyre's wear, so even a slope stable by
  construction could not reach a within-stint R-squared of 0.2 at 16 of 22
  Formula 1 circuits, and measured directly as a variance component the slope
  moves between races by about a third of its mean. Replaying 1,280 real first
  pit stops with each team's next stop held fixed, a time-optimal planner lands
  a median of +3 laps from the Formula 1 team out of sample, but a rule that
  only balances tyre age before that stop does as well in every class. Read as
  an implied wear ratio, teams' stint lengths follow compound hardness in both
  directions of change (0.73 to a harder compound, 1.74 to a softer one) where
  the fitted slopes are no better than chance, and only partly better once
  each compound is given its own baseline pace.

  Every quantity is measured from published timing or absent; nothing is
  imputed. Two estimators built for this work were validated on synthetic data
  and then withdrawn when they failed on real fields, and those withdrawals are
  described rather than omitted.
keywords:
  - motorsport
  - race strategy
  - Formula 1
  - endurance racing
  - tyre degradation
  - dynamic programming
  - Monte Carlo simulation
  - model validation
  - reproducibility
